XML injection vulnerability: attribute values are not encoded, allowing injection of malicious XML. Use `xmlEncode` to escape special characters.

```
	for (var attr in attributes){
		elem.append(attr + '="');
		elem.append(dojox.xmpp.util.xmlEncode(attributes[attr]));
		elem.append('" ');
	}
```

XSS vulnerability: encoding name `${enc}` is not HTML-escaped before insertion into the DOM. If `IO_FORMAT` keys ever contain HTML special characters like `<`, `>`, `"`, or `'`, this could allow script injection.

To fix, HTML-escape the `enc` variable:
```
const escapedEnc = enc.replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;').replace(/>/g, '&gt;').replace(/"/g, '&quot;').replace(/'/g, '&#x27;');
table += `<tr><td>${escapedEnc}</td><td>${value}</td></tr>`;
```

The `continue` only skips the current keyPart but doesn't break the entire key processing. If `__proto__` appears in the middle of a path like `foo.__proto__.bar`, it will skip setting `__proto__` but may still create unsafe references in `current`.

Consider breaking the outer loop entirely when an invalid key is detected:

```
// Ensure we don't allow prototype pollution
if (INVALID_KEYS.includes(k)) {
  break; // Break out of keyParts loop entirely
}
```

prototype pollution vulnerability (CVE-2020-8192): directly copying properties from `headers` without filtering dangerous keys like `__proto__`, `constructor`, or `prototype` allows attackers to pollute Object prototypes

```
Object.keys(headers).forEach(k => { 
  if (!['__proto__', 'constructor', 'prototype'].includes(k)) {
    headersSchemaLowerCase[k] = headers[k] 
  }
})
```
